WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.477
Constraints are powerful, but adding them to an existing database requires care.

00:00:05.477 --> 00:00:08.482
First, profile the current data.

00:00:08.482 --> 00:00:13.852
You may discover that many historical records already violate the proposed rule.

00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:18.832
Next, document allowed values and important exceptions.

00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:23.162
Then decide how existing violations should be handled.

00:00:23.162 --> 00:00:25.832
Some may be corrected using evidence.

00:00:25.832 --> 00:00:28.553
Others may need to be quarantined or reviewed.

00:00:29.003 --> 00:00:32.205
Also consider the systems that insert data.

00:00:32.205 --> 00:00:39.650
A new constraint can break an existing import process if the incoming data does not satisfy the rule.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:47.770
So the sequence matters: understand the data, confirm the business rule, address legacy problems, test the impact,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.915
and then enforce the stable rule.

00:00:51.915 --> 00:00:58.285
Constraints are most effective when they are the final step of a well-understood quality policy,

00:00:58.285 --> 00:01:00.855
not the first reaction to messy data.
